<article dtd-version="1.3" article-type="meeting-abstract" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="hwp">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">ASCOMTG</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Clinical Oncology</journal-title><abbrev-journal-title>ASCO MEETING ABSTRACTS</abbrev-journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0732-183X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1527-7755</issn><publisher><publisher-name>American Society of Clinical Oncology</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="meeting-id">335</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-id">17304</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-participation-id">8001600</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="temporary-abstract-id">55592</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="abstract-id">rw55592</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.18632/oncotarget.22952</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Biochemistry</subject></subj-group><series-title>2026 ASCO Annual Meeting (May 29 - June 2, 2026)</series-title><series-text>Publication Only</series-text></article-categories><title-group><article-title>IGF-1 induces the epithelial-mesenchymal transition via Stat5 in hepatocellular carcinoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib id="C8001600" contrib-type="presenter"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>Chuanzong</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001601" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Qian</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001602" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Ben</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001603" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sun</surname><given-names>Qi</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001604" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>He</surname><given-names>Zhaobin</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001605" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hong</surname><given-names>Jianguo</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001606" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kuehn</surname><given-names>Florian</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001607" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>Enyu</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8001608" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>Zongli</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8001600" /></contrib><aff id="A8001600">Department of General Surgery, Qilu Hospital of Shandong University, Jinan, P.R. China; Department of Cardiology, Shandong Provincial Hospital Affiliated to Shandong University, Jinan, P.R. China; Department of General, Thoracic, Vascular and Transplantation Surgery, University of Rostock, Rostock, Germany;</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2017</year></pub-date><fpage>rw55592</fpage><lpage>rw55592</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>ASCO-style conversion for internal testing; source rights remain with the original publisher.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2017</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p><bold>rw55592</bold></p><p>It has been reported that the epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) plays an important role in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC). However, the relationship between the insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) and EMT of HCC was not fully elucidated. In the present work, we found that the expression of N-cadherin, Vimentin, Snail1, Snail2, and Twist1 was positively associated with IGF-1R expression, while E-cadherin expression was negatively associated with IGF-1 expression in human HCC samples. Furthermore, we observed that IGF-1 up-regulated the expression of N-cadherin, Vimentin, Snail1, Snail2 and Twist1, and down-regulated the expression of E-cadherin. In addition, Stat5 was induced in IGF-1-treated HepG2 and Hep3B cells, and Stat5 inhibition or siRNA significantly affected IGF-1-induced EMT in HepG2 and Hep3B cells. In conclusion, IGF-1 induces EMT of HCC via Stat5 signaling pathway. Thus, IGF-1/Stat5 can be recommended as a potential and novel therapeutic strategy for HCC patients.</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-row-number</meta-name><meta-value>16</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-record-id</meta-name><meta-value>55592.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-journal</meta-name><meta-value>Oncotarget</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-publisher</meta-name><meta-value>Impact Journals</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-country</meta-name><meta-value>China;Germany</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-article-type</meta-name><meta-value>Research Article;</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>manual-verdict</meta-name><meta-value>YES</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-reason</meta-name><meta-value>Duplication of/in Image;Investigation by Journal/Publisher;Investigation by Third Party;Paper Mill;</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front></article>
